---
import { Badge } from '@/components/ui/badge';
import Base from '@/layouts/Base.astro';

const repo = 'https://github.com/hakula139/assistant-bench';
const docs = `${repo}/blob/main/docs/design`;

const sections = {
  rounds: 'How a round works',
  scores: 'Scores and intervals',
  leaderboard: 'Reading the leaderboard',
  leader: 'How the leader is chosen',
  summary: 'Round summary',
  tests: 'Boards scored by tests',
  limits: 'Known limits',
} as const;

const columns = [
  {
    term: 'Rank',
    definition:
      'From 1 plus the systems whose interval lies wholly above this one, to 1 plus those whose interval reaches above its lower end.',
  },
  {
    term: 'Preliminary',
    badge: true,
    definition:
      'Fewer than 40 decisive votes, those won by either side, so the score may still move.',
  },
  {
    term: 'Unranked',
    definition:
      'Listed below the table without a score until enough comparisons connect it to the reference.',
  },
  {
    term: 'Votes',
    definition: "The rater's votes on the system, leaving out results settled without a vote.",
  },
  {
    term: 'Both bad',
    definition: 'The share of its votes judged both bad.',
  },
  {
    term: 'Gate failures',
    definition: 'The share of its outputs that failed a gate.',
  },
  {
    term: 'Cost / output and p50 latency',
    definition: 'The mean cost at list prices and the median time to the complete response.',
  },
];

const diagnostics = [
  {
    term: 'Rater agreement',
    definition:
      'How often a side-swapped repeat matched the original vote. Below 70%, the intervals overstate how well systems separate.',
  },
  {
    term: 'Separated pairs',
    definition: 'The share of system pairs whose intervals do not overlap.',
  },
  {
    term: 'Split-half τ',
    definition:
      "Kendall's τ between the orders fit to odd and to even sessions, where 1 means the same order.",
  },
  {
    term: 'Left out of the fit',
    definition:
      'Votes on a retired case, on an output since removed or now failing a gate, or on a pair the outputs now settle.',
  },
];

const h2 = 'scroll-mt-6 text-lg font-semibold target:text-link';
const dl = 'grid gap-y-4 sm:grid-cols-[9rem_1fr] sm:gap-x-6 sm:gap-y-2';
const list = 'space-y-2 pl-6 marker:text-muted-foreground';
---

<Base title="Methodology">
  <div class="grid gap-8 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,42rem)_13rem] lg:justify-between lg:gap-x-16 lg:gap-y-10">
    <header class="space-y-3 lg:col-start-1">
      <h1 class="text-[1.75rem] leading-tight font-semibold">Methodology</h1>
      <p class="leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
        Every system in a family answers the same cases with the same instruction, and one rater
        compares their outputs in blind pairs.
      </p>
    </header>

    <nav
      aria-label="On this page"
      class="text-sm lg:sticky lg:top-8 lg:col-start-2 lg:row-span-2 lg:row-start-1 lg:self-start"
    >
      <p class="mb-2 font-medium">On this page</p>
      <ul class="space-y-1.5">
        {Object.entries(sections).map(([id, title]) => (
          <li>
            <a href={`#${id}`} class="text-muted-foreground hover:text-foreground">
              {title}
            </a>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </nav>

    <article class="space-y-10 leading-relaxed lg:col-start-1">
      <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 id="rounds" class={h2}>
          {sections.rounds}
        </h2>
        <ol class:list={['list-decimal', list]}>
          <li>
            A family is one kind of task with its own cases and leaderboard. A system is a model
            with its effort, harness, and prompt, and changing any part makes a new system.
          </li>
          <li>
            Gates check each output for problems such as truncation or the wrong script. A failing
            output loses without a vote, and two identical outputs tie.
          </li>
          <li>
            The rater picks the better output, a tie, or both bad, in sessions of up to 30 pairs,
            with sides counterbalanced.
          </li>
          <li>Each round is a dated snapshot that refits every vote cast so far.</li>
        </ol>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 id="scores" class={h2}>
          {sections.scores}
        </h2>
        <p>
          A Bradley-Terry model turns the votes and the results settled without a vote into Elo-like
          scores, counting ties and both-bad votes as half a win. Each family's reference system
          sits at 1,000, so scores compare only within a family. A 100-point gap means the higher
          system should take about 64% of the votes between them.
        </p>
        <p>
          The 95% intervals come from 1,000 bootstrap resamples of whole cases, since votes on one
          case are correlated.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 id="leaderboard" class={h2}>
          {sections.leaderboard}
        </h2>
        <p>
          The plot draws each 95% interval on one axis, with a line at the reference score and
          hollow dots for preliminary systems.
        </p>
        <dl class={dl}>
          {columns.map(({ term, badge, definition }) => (
            <div class="sm:contents">
              <dt class="font-medium">
                {badge ? (
                  <Badge variant="outline" className="font-normal text-muted-foreground">
                    {term}
                  </Badge>
                ) : (
                  term
                )}
              </dt>
              <dd>{definition}</dd>
            </div>
          ))}
        </dl>
        <p>
          A dash marks a missing value. A category view refits that category's votes alone, so
          intervals widen and Rank disappears, while the columns labeled "all categories" stay
          family-wide.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 id="leader" class={h2}>
          {sections.leader}
        </h2>
        <ol class:list={['list-decimal', list]}>
          <li>
            A ranked system ties for the top when the 95% interval of its score difference from the
            top system, paired within each resample, contains 0 or is unbounded. Pairing can
            separate two systems whose intervals overlap.
          </li>
          <li>
            While any system tied for the top, the top one included, is preliminary, the round names
            no leader.
          </li>
          <li>
            Otherwise a lone top system leads, and a tie goes to the lower cost per output, then the
            lower median latency, then the higher score, with a known value beating a missing one.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 id="summary" class={h2}>
          {sections.summary}
        </h2>
        <dl class={dl}>
          {diagnostics.map(({ term, definition }) => (
            <div class="sm:contents">
              <dt class="font-medium">{term}</dt>
              <dd>{definition}</dd>
            </div>
          ))}
        </dl>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 id="tests" class={h2}>
          {sections.tests}
        </h2>
        <p>
          Some families score each trial, one run of a system on a task, by automated tests. Pass
          rate counts errored trials as failures, and Cost / trial and p50 latency are a trial's
          mean cost and median time.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 id="limits" class={h2}>
          {sections.limits}
        </h2>
        <ul class:list={['list-disc', list]}>
          <li>One person casts every vote, so each board reflects that person's preferences.</li>
          <li>
            Scores are relative, so the both-bad and gate-failure rates are the only absolute
            signals.
          </li>
          <li>
            Scores carry no length adjustment. Each system page reports whether the rater favors
            longer or shorter outputs.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p class="text-muted-foreground">
          The design notes on <a href={`${docs}/ranking.md`}>ranking</a>, the{' '}
          <a href={`${docs}/arena.md`}>vote arena</a>, and each{' '}
          <a href={`${repo}/tree/main/docs/design/families`}>family</a> cover the math.
        </p>
      </section>
    </article>
  </div>
</Base>
